---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import { anchorPlacementsTree } from "../demos/anchoring";
import ComponentPreview from "../components/ComponentPreview.astro";
import StylingHooks from "../components/StylingHooks.astro";

const import_ = `@import "@skryensya/core/patterns/anchored.css";`;

const anatomy = `<!-- The ANCHOR: whatever it is you measure against. Almost always the trigger. -->
<button class="sk-button sk-interactive sk-anchor" data-sk-anchor-trigger>
  Save
</button>

<!-- The POSITIONER: only placement, never painting, never clicking. -->
<span class="sk-tooltip__positioner sk-anchored" data-sk-placement="block-start">
  <!-- The ARROW, optional. Inside, but placed against the ANCHOR. -->
  <span class="sk-anchored-arrow" aria-hidden="true"></span>
  <!-- The CONTENT: what you see. -->
  <span class="sk-tooltip__content" data-sk-anchor-content>
    Saves only every 30 seconds
  </span>
</span>`;



const escapeHatch = `/* A submenu appears on the side and aligned above: it is not any of the four.
   It is requested by the hook, not by adding a fifth to the public vocabulary. */
.sk-menu__positioner[data-sk-submenu] {
  --sk-anchored-position-area: inline-end span-block-end;
  --sk-anchored-justify: auto;
  --sk-anchored-align: start;
  --sk-anchored-position-try: flip-inline;
}`;

const sameWidth = `/* The listbox measures what its trigger measures. Goes inside the @supports:
   anchor-size() without a resolved anchor is invalid. */
@supports (anchor-name: --a) {
  .sk-select__positioner {
    --sk-anchored-size: anchor-size(width);
  }
}`;

const arrow = `<span class="sk-tooltip__positioner sk-anchored" data-sk-placement="block-start">
  <span class="sk-anchored-arrow" aria-hidden="true"></span>
  <span class="sk-tooltip__content">…</span>
</span>`;

const arrowReact = `<Tooltip arrow content="It keeps itself">
  <button className="sk-button sk-interactive">Save</button>
</Tooltip>

<Popover arrow trigger="View profile" title="Ada Lovelace">
  Mathematician and writer.
</Popover>`;

---

<Base
  title="Anchoring"
  documentLayout="grid"
  description="A pattern: placing a box against another element, with the browser as the engine and the machine as the fallback."
>
  <h1>Anchoring</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    A box placed against <strong>another</strong> element, not against the page: it opens from one side of the anchor, flips to the opposite side when it does not fit, and disappears if the anchor leaves the screen. This is what tooltip, popover, popup, menu and its submenus, select, combobox and date picker do.
  </p>

  <ComponentPreview tree={anchorPlacementsTree} label="HTML" />

  <h2>Why is it a pattern and not a component</h2>
  <p>
    The rule is a single question: <em>could a second component need this exact structure?</em> Nothing has to be predicted here, <strong>nine already need it</strong>. So anchoring ships <strong>hooks and structure</strong>, the way <a href="/vaul">Vaul</a> does, not just variables.
  </p>
  <p>
    Do not confuse it with Vaul, though both talk about anchoring: a Vaul anchors to an <strong>edge of the viewport</strong>, this anchors to <strong>another element</strong>.
  </p>

  <h2>The three parts</h2>
  <p>
    Two flat classes that <strong>compose</strong> with a component's own parts, the way <code>sk-interactive</code> composes with <code>sk-button</code>, plus an optional third. They are not a BEM pair because the anchor is not inside the positioner, and in React the positioner is ported to <code>&lt;body&gt;</code>: nesting one under the other would name a containment that does not exist.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={anatomy} label="HTML" />
  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>sk-anchor</code>, the <strong>anchor</strong>: whatever the panel is measured against. Almost always the trigger, but never automatically, because anchoring is a geometric role and opening is an interactive one. Combobox and DatePicker anchor the <em>whole</em> control, since the list lines up with the field being typed into and not with the 32px chevron beside it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>sk-anchored</code>, the <strong>positioner</strong>. It carries the placement and nothing else: it does not paint and it does not intercept the pointer, so the invisible box that it reserves cannot block what is below. Its child, the content, is what you see and what you click.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>sk-anchored-arrow</code>, the <strong>arrow</strong>, optional. It is authored or it does not exist. It goes
      inside the positioner, and is still placed against the <strong>anchor</strong>, not against the box.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The browser does the placing</h2>
  <p>
    Where the <em>CSS Anchor Positioning</em> API exists, the browser does the placing: no layout loop, no measuring on every scroll, with flipping and a vanished anchor resolved inside the layout engine, which is where that information already lives. Where it does not, the Zag machine places it.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <strong>fallback is not a lesser path</strong>, it is the same contract run by a different engine. What it does require is that the two never run at once: on the browser path the binding strips the inline <code>style</code> the machine writes off the positioner. That is also why the pattern's own declarations do without <code>!important</code>, and the omission is structural rather than sloppy: <code>position-try</code> can only flip declarations that are <strong>not</strong> <code>!important</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Four placements, on logical axes</h2>
  <p>
    <code>block-start</code>, <code>block-end</code>, <code>inline-start</code> and <code>inline-end</code>, requested with <code>data-sk-placement</code> on the <strong>positioner</strong> and not on the root, because in React the positioner is ported and the inheritance from the root does not reach there. They flip on their own in RTL.
  </p>
  <p>
    It is a <strong>preference, not a guarantee</strong>: if it does not fit, the browser flips it to the opposite side of <em>the same axis</em>. Whoever asks for <code>inline-end</code> wants the box beside the anchor, and landing on top of it would be disobeying, not adapting.
  </p>

  <h2>Rare cases use the hook, not a fifth placement</h2>
  <p>
    The vocabulary is not enlarged by exceptions. A submenu wants to appear on the side aligned above, and that is requested by overwriting the hook directly, which is what a hook is for. Letting the exception write the contract is how a vocabulary of four becomes one of nine.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={escapeHatch} label="CSS" />
  <p>
    The other hook that gets real use is width. Select and Combobox measure what their anchor measures:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={sameWidth} label="CSS" />

  <h2>The arrow is optional, and is authored</h2>
  <p>
    A square rotated 45° that peeks over the edge of the box toward the anchor. If the markup does not include <code>.sk-anchored-arrow</code>, there is no arrow, and that is the default. A <a href="/components/tooltip">tooltip</a> or a <a href="/components/popover">popover</a> want one because they are floating chrome that has to say <em>which control</em> it is talking about; a menu or a select do not, because there the shared border already states the relationship.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={arrow} label="HTML" />
  <p>In React it is a prop, turned off by default:</p>
  <CodeBlock code={arrowReact} label="React" />
  <p>
    It is <strong>decorative</strong>, so it always carries <code>aria-hidden</code>: it adds no
    information, it repeats what the placement already says.
  </p>

  <h3>It comes from the anchor, not from the center of the box</h3>
  <p>
    The difference shows the moment the box moves: <code>anchor-center</code> centres it on the <em>anchor</em> but pushes it back on screen if it does not fit, so a trigger near the edge leaves the box displaced, and an arrow drawn at 50% of that box ends up pointing anywhere but at the control. So the arrow is not placed against the box: it is <strong>a second box anchored</strong> to the same anchor, with the same <code>position-area</code> and the same flip.
  </p>
  <p>
    And it still <strong>lives inside</strong> the positioner, which ought to be impossible: the spec only lets you anchor to something that is a <em>descendant of the element's containing block</em>, and the trigger does not hang off the positioner. <code>position: fixed</code> is what solves it: a fixed element's containing block is the <strong>viewport</strong>, where the trigger does live, even though in the DOM the arrow is still inside. With <code>absolute</code> the containing block would be the positioner, and <code>anchor()</code> and <code>anchor-center</code> would be invalid <em>silently</em>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Staying inside is not fussiness either, it is what three separate things need at once. A <a href="/components/popover">Popover</a>'s box goes to the <em>top layer</em> when it opens, and it only gets in there with its children. As a positioned child, the arrow paints <strong>over</strong> the box's background and edge, which is what makes the seam disappear. And in the <strong>fallback</strong> the machine does the placing, and it looks for the arrow inside the floating element.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>It flips together with the box</em> because it takes the box's own height as an outward margin (<code>anchor-size()</code>): the two have the same footprint, so they cross the <code>position-try</code> threshold at the same moment.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>What the cost comes down to:</strong> when flipping, <code>position-try</code> flips margins and alignment but not <code>rotate</code>, which is not a property it accepts. The rhombus is symmetrical at 180°, so the shape does not change, only which of its sides carry the border does: invisible on a tooltip's borderless arrow, a line on the wrong side once a border is painted.
  </p>

  <h2>Who uses it</h2>
  <p>
    Seven components compose <code>sk-anchored</code> today. The last column says who resolves the placement: almost all of them stay on the browser route, and the only one that also carries machine positioning is the tooltip.
  </p>
  <div class="sk-table-scroll">
    <table class="sk-table">
      <thead class="sk-table__head">
        <tr class="sk-table__row">
          <th scope="col" class="sk-table__header">Component</th>
          <th scope="col" class="sk-table__header">What it anchors to</th>
          <th scope="col" class="sk-table__header">Arrow</th>
          <th scope="col" class="sk-table__header">Placed by</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody class="sk-table__body">
        <tr class="sk-table__row">
          <td class="sk-table__cell"><a href="/components/tooltip">Tooltip</a></td>
          <td class="sk-table__cell">the trigger</td>
          <td class="sk-table__cell">yes</td>
          <td class="sk-table__cell">browser + machine (Zag)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="sk-table__row">
          <td class="sk-table__cell"><a href="/components/popover">Popover</a></td>
          <td class="sk-table__cell">the trigger</td>
          <td class="sk-table__cell">yes</td>
          <td class="sk-table__cell">browser</td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="sk-table__row">
          <td class="sk-table__cell"><a href="/components/popover#popup">Popup</a></td>
          <td class="sk-table__cell">the trigger</td>
          <td class="sk-table__cell">yes, opt-in</td>
          <td class="sk-table__cell">browser</td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="sk-table__row">
          <td class="sk-table__cell"><a href="/components/menu">Menu</a> and its submenus</td>
          <td class="sk-table__cell">the trigger; the parent item in the submenu</td>
          <td class="sk-table__cell">no</td>
          <td class="sk-table__cell">browser</td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="sk-table__row">
          <td class="sk-table__cell"><a href="/components/select">Select</a></td>
          <td class="sk-table__cell">the whole control</td>
          <td class="sk-table__cell">no</td>
          <td class="sk-table__cell">browser</td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="sk-table__row">
          <td class="sk-table__cell"><a href="/components/combobox">Combobox</a></td>
          <td class="sk-table__cell">the whole control</td>
          <td class="sk-table__cell">no</td>
          <td class="sk-table__cell">browser</td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="sk-table__row">
          <td class="sk-table__cell"><a href="/components/date-picker">DatePicker</a></td>
          <td class="sk-table__cell">the whole control</td>
          <td class="sk-table__cell">no</td>
          <td class="sk-table__cell">browser</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    The two cuts in the table are the arguments from above, already applied. The arrow belongs to the three that are <strong>floating chrome</strong> and have to say which control they are talking about; menu, select and combobox do not, because there the border shared with the anchor already states the relationship. And the ones that anchor the <em>whole control</em> are the ones you type into: the list lines up with the field, not with the 32px chevron beside it.
  </p>

  <h2>Install</h2>
  <p>
    It is opt-in, like every pattern: import it before the components that consume it.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={import_} label="CSS" />

  <StylingHooks groups={[{ file: "patterns/anchored.css", probe: "sk-anchored" }]} />
</Base>

<style is:global>
  /*
   * WHAT IS LEFT FOR THE PAGE, which is the STAGE and not the component. How the demo is composed -
   * a padded Box around a centred row with a gap - is the tree's (`demos/anchoring.ts`), so the
   * snippet a reader copies behaves like the one on this page. These three are about the box this
   * demo is being SHOWN in, and a reader copying the code should not inherit any of them.
   *
   * `is:global`: the authored HTML lives inside the srcdoc and does not carry Astro's scope
   * attribute. The class sits on the element itself in both bindings, so one selector reaches the
   * Vanilla stage and the React one alike (a rule written against `frame-body > *` would not: React
   * mounts one `display: contents` wrapper deeper).
   */
  .sk-anchoring-demo {
    /* The stage is a wrapping flex ROW, so a specimen is sized by its content and lands at the start
       edge: the Box has to claim the line before anything inside it can be centred in the frame. */
    inline-size: 100%;
  }

  /*
   * CLEARANCE FOR THE SIDE PLACEMENTS, which is the one thing the gap scale cannot say.
   *
   * `inline-start` and `inline-end` open beside the trigger, and the tooltip is ~60px wide plus the
   * pattern's own 8px offset: at the scale's widest gap (`xl`, 32px) the box opened straight over the
   * neighbouring button and the demo looked broken - which is exactly what it was showing, four
   * tooltips crowded into a row nobody would ship. 5rem is the measured clearance, re-declared
   * through the hook the pattern publishes for it rather than by overriding a layout rule.
   *
   * It stays here rather than in the tree because it is about showing FOUR at once: a real row shows
   * one tooltip at a time, and the tree's `gap="xl"` is the right answer for that.
   */
  .sk-anchoring-demo > .sk-inline {
    --sk-inline-gap: 5rem;
    /*
     * TALLER THAN THE DEMO NEEDS, on purpose, and floored on the ROW rather than on the Box around
     * it: the row is already a flex line with `align-items: center` (the tree asks for it), so the
     * height it is given is height the triggers sit in the middle of. On the Box the same number
     * parked them at the top with a quarter of the stage empty underneath.
     *
     * A floor, not a height: the frame still reports its content size, so nothing is clipped when
     * the row wraps at a narrow width.
     */
    min-block-size: 16rem;
  }
  /*
   * THE DEMO SHOWS THE PLACEMENT IT NAMES, so the fallback is off for these four.
   *
   * Every anchored box in this system flips when it does not fit (`--sk-anchored-position-try`), and
   * that is right everywhere except here: a row labelled `block-end` that renders above the trigger
   * is teaching the reader the wrong word. The flip has its own section on this page, with its own
   * prose; the specimen at the top has to be literal.
   *
   * It also routes around a real disagreement between the two bindings, measured on this page: in
   * React one of the four (the `block-end` one) comes out INVERTED - whatever side it asks for, Blink
   * applies `flip-block` and renders the opposite, with 138px of unused room on the side it refused.
   * Re-tagging that same element to `block-start` renders it block-end, and its three siblings behave
   * correctly, so it is not the placement and not the room. Vanilla, same markup, same stage, places
   * all four correctly. Written down here rather than left as a mystery: with no fallback to apply,
   * both bindings show the same four sides.
   *
   * NOT scoped under `.sk-anchoring-demo`, and that is not an oversight: React portals every
   * positioner to the body, so it is not inside the demo any more. This page renders exactly one
   * preview, and these styles are copied only into that preview's frames, so naming the part is
   * enough.
   *
   * ONE HOOK REACHES THE ARROW TOO, which it did not until this page asked for it: the arrow's own
   * rules hard-coded `flip-block`/`flip-inline` while the box read `--sk-anchored-position-try`, so
   * turning the flip off here left the box where it was asked and the arrow still flipping - a
   * diamond stranded on the far side of the trigger. The pattern now reads the same hook on both,
   * with each axis's flip as its default (`patterns/anchored.css`), which is what its own comment
   * ("what makes the two FLIP TOGETHER") always meant.
   */
  .sk-component-preview__frame-body .sk-anchored[data-sk-placement] {
    --sk-anchored-position-try: none;
  }
</style>
